winex11: Let the server auto-repeat held keys, so modifier keys repeat too.

MapleStory TW binds skills to Shift / Ctrl / Alt and expects them to fire
again and again while held, exactly as J or any other key does.  On Windows
they do: the keyboard's typematic repeat covers every key, modifiers
included.  Under X they never repeat, whatever `xset r` says, because the X
server refuses in two places (xserver, also Xwayland 24.1):

    _XkbFilterSetState()            xkb/xkbActions.c
        if (filter->keycode == 0) { /* initial press */
            AccessXCancelRepeatKey(xkbi, keycode);

  Soft repeat: AccessXFilterPressEvent() starts the repeat timer for the key
  when its per-key bit is set, and the SetMods/SetGroup action every
  modifier key carries cancels it again on the same press.

    GetKeyboardEvents()             dix/getevents.c
        /* ... or we have a modifier, don't generate a repeat event. */
        if (... || pDev->key->xkbInfo->desc->map->modmap[key_code]) return 0;

  Hard repeat: a second press of a held key that is in the modifier map is
  dropped, so a compositor (gamescope) re-sending the press cannot help.

Measured under gamescope's Xwayland with a passive XI2 listener: J held 2.1s
gives 114 X repeat events, Left Shift held 1.9s gives none, with the Shift
bits of the autorepeat bitmap on for both the core and the slave keyboard.

wineserver already implements Windows-style repeat (server/queue.c, the
desktop's key_repeat: the last key pressed repeats, KeyboardDelay /
KeyboardSpeed from the registry); only winewayland turns it on, because
Wayland sends no repeats.  Turn it on in winex11 as well, and make the server
the only source of repeats: a hardware key-down for a key that is already
down, and not coming from the server's own repeat timer, is dropped.
Otherwise X's repeats and the server's interleave -- with X's delay (600ms
under gamescope by default) longer than the server's (500ms) a held J got a
burst of server repeats at 12ms before X's 40ms repeats took over.

Injected input (SendInput) is not affected: the repeat
logic only looks at IMO_HARDWARE.

keyrepeat-probe in a separate prefix under gamescope, 2s holds:
                 before           after
    Left Shift   1 WM_KEYDOWN     133, first repeat 501ms, then 12ms
    Ctrl         1                157 (with the first half of this patch)
    Alt          1                161 WM_SYSKEYDOWN, 498ms / 12ms
    J            X's 600/40ms     183, 501ms / 12ms
    Shift, then J held 398ms later: Shift once, then J repeats (as Windows).

diff --git a/server/queue.c b/server/queue.c
--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -2369,6 +2369,11 @@
         break;
     }
 
+    /* when the server auto-repeats, it is the only source of repeats: drop the driver's own */
+    if (origin == IMO_HARDWARE && !repeat && desktop->key_repeat.enable &&
+        !(input->kbd.flags & KEYEVENTF_KEYUP) && (desktop_shm->keystate[vkey] & 0x80))
+        return 0;
+
     message_code = (input->kbd.flags & KEYEVENTF_KEYUP) ? WM_KEYUP : WM_KEYDOWN;
     switch (vkey)
     {
diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/x11drv_main.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/x11drv_main.c
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/x11drv_main.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/x11drv_main.c
@@ -756,6 +756,9 @@
 
     XkbUseExtension( data->display, NULL, NULL );
     XkbSetDetectableAutoRepeat( data->display, True, NULL );
+    /* X never auto-repeats modifier keys, so let the server repeat held keys the Windows way;
+     * the server then drops the repeats X does send for other keys. */
+    NtUserCallOneParam( TRUE, NtUserCallOneParam_SetKeyboardAutoRepeat );
     if (TRACE_ON(synchronous)) XSynchronize( data->display, True );
 
     set_queue_display_fd( data->display );
